    "lawCode": "Amd §202, V & T L",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5668\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to consent\nfor the use of registration lists and title information\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nTo better protect the privacy of information contained in motor\nvehicle records.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThe bill would require that information contained in motor vehicle\nregistration files and sold to the highest responsible bidder(s) be\ndeleted from such file for all purposes other than motor vehicle\nwarranty and safety recall notices and statistical compilations,\nunless the registrant has provided his or her express written consent.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\n\nParagraph (b) of subdivision 4 of § 201 of the Vehicle and Traffic Law\nauthorizes the inclusion of registration information in the file sold\nto the highest responsible bidder unless the registrant indicates his\nor her desire to have such information deleted.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nUnder current law, the Department of Motor Vehicles is authorized to\ncontract with the highest bidder for the sale of registration\ninformation contained in the records of all registrations from any\nregistration period. The term of the contract is limited to two years,\nand the sale of the information must be limited to only that portion\nof the registration files describing the name and address of the\nregistered owner of the vehicle, and the make, model, year, weight,\nbody style, number of passengers and cylinders, fuel, license number,\ntype of registration and transaction, validation and expiration date,\nand vehicle identification number.\n\nThe law requires DMV to notify each vehicle registrant of the sale of\nthis information, and provide them with a way to delete their\ninformation for all purposes other than those relating to motor\nvehicle warranty, safety recall and similar notices, and for\nstatistical compilations. However, individuals often still do not know\nthat the State is selling their personal information and therefore do\nnot exercise their option to prevent the use of their information.\nRequiring that individuals must give their express consent to the sale\nof this information would guarantee that people who are unaware of the\npractice would automatically achieve deletion, but provide those who\nwould be interested in receiving direct mail or other types of\ncommercial solicitations with a method to indicate their desire to\nmake their personal information available.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2013-14. A.1145 Referred to Transportation\n\n\n2011-12: A.172 Referred to Transportation\n2009-10: A.517 Referred to Transportation\n2007-08. A.78 Referred to Transportation\n2005-06: A.3628 Referred to Transportation\n2003-04: A.324 Reported/Referred to Ways and Means\n2001-02: A.41 Passed Assembly\n1999-00: A.8110-A Passed Assembly\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nUndetermined potential loss of revenue if individuals decide to\nwithhold their express consent.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\n120 days after enactment.\n\n",
